    "answer": {
      "short": "$y=\\dfrac{e^2}{x}$",
      "worked": "The original equation requires $x>0$. Use $2=\\ln(e^2)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\ln(y)&=\\ln(e^2)-2\\ln(x)+\\ln(x)\\\\\\ln(y)&=\\ln(e^2)-\\ln(x)\\\\\\ln(y)&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{e^2}{x}\\right)\\\\y&=\\frac{e^2}{x}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
